        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Toxicity secondary to the overdose of a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI), serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI), or coingestion of two types of serotonergic drugs.",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "SSRIs include citalopram (Celexa®), escitalopram (Lexapro®), fluoxetine (Prozac®), fluvoxamine (Luvox®), paroxetine (Paxil®), sertraline (Zoloft®), vilazodone (Viibryd®), and vortioxetine (Brintellix®). SNRIs include desvenlafaxine (Pristiq®), duloxetine (Cymbalta®), levomilnacipran (Fetzima®), milnacipran (Ixel®, Savella®), tofenacin (Elamol®, Tofacine®), and venlafaxine (Effexor®).",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "SSRIs and SNRIs are antidepressants that inhibit reuptake of serotonin, a neuro transmitter involved in aggression, anxiety, appetite, depression, migraine, pain, and sleep. Antidepressant effects are secondary to inhibition of reuptake of serotonin into serotonergic neurons via binding to the serotonin transporter, resulting in increased synaptic availability of serotonin. SNRIs also inhibit the reuptake of norepinephrine by the same mechanism.",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Excessive stimulation of serotonin receptors can occur by enhanced serotonin synthesis, increased presynaptic serotonin release, inhibition of serotonin uptake into the presynaptic neuron, inhibition of serotonin metabolism, or serotonin agonism. Ingesting too much of one medication or a combination of medications that increase serotonin via different methods can lead to serotonin syndrome.",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Serotonin syndrome is characterized in humans as a combination of symptoms that include at least three of the following: myoclonus, mental aberration, agitation, hyperreflexia, tremors, diarrhea, ataxia, or hyperthermia.",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Toxic dosage varies widely among commonly available SSRIs and SNRIs and is not well defined in veterinary medicine.",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cardiovascular—decreased vascular tone (hypotension), increased heart rate and stroke volume (tachycardia).",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Gastrointestinal—increased smooth muscle contractility (vomiting, diarrhea).",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Nervous—stimulation (agitation, restlessness, seizures) and altered mental status (vocalization, disorientation).",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Neuromuscular—autonomic dysfunction (hyperactivity) and neuromuscular hyperactivity (hyperreflexia, myoclonus, tremors).",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ophthalmic—increased autonomic function (mydriasis).",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Respiratory—increased bronchial smooth muscle contraction (dyspnea).",
              "pdf_page": 184,
              "printed_page": 102,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
